<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">America the Beautiful</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem written <a href="/years/1893">1893</a>, published 4 July <a href="/years/1895">1895</a>; tune <a href="/years/1882">1882</a>; first published together <a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In 1893 Katharine Lee Bates, a 33-year-old English professor at Wellesley College, took the train to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Springs to teach at Colorado College. Sights on the journey found their way into her poem: the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago, the &quot;White City&quot; whose gleaming buildings promised a future; the wheat fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, through which her train passed on 16 July; and the view of the Great Plains from the top of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">Pikes</a> Peak.</p></div>
